# LicenX — Universal Cross-IDE AI Rules

**CRITICAL RULE: This is a multi-IDE project. Whenever any modification, update, or bug fix is completed in our session, you must automatically update the `MEMORY.md` file or provide the exact markdown blocks so the project context remains perfectly synchronized across all IDEs.**

## Read-first contract

1. Before project changes, read `AGENTS.md`, `AI_INSTRUCTIONS.md`, and root `MEMORY.md`. Consult current source/manifests, `git status`, and relevant private notes only when access permits. These repository instructions apply to VS Code, Google Antigravity, GitHub Copilot and any other agent that reads them.
2. Treat `MEMORY.md` as the shared, tracked, source-backed project orientation, **not** an automatic transcript or guaranteed IDE memory service. Newly opened IDEs must load the latest checked-out commit and these rule files. Source and current Git/CI/runtime evidence outrank stale memory entries.
3. After **each authorized** code, configuration, feature, security, infrastructure, or bug-fix change, update the relevant `MEMORY.md` section **in the same completed session**. Record (a) what changed and exact paths, (b) tested behavior and test/CI results, (c) current version/commit/deployment scope, and (d) known limitations and the next approved gate. Make small factual edits; preserve dated history without claiming old snapshots are current.
4. If memory cannot be safely written (no editor/permissions, conflicting changes, unverified facts), explicitly provide the **exact ready-to-paste Markdown block** and location, mark the repository update as pending, and do not claim it was synchronized.
5. Never put credentials, tokens, license keys, recovery keys, personally identifying data or local forensic material in tracked `MEMORY.md`. Keep `notes/` private and Git-ignored. Do not overwrite other agents' or the user's unsaved work.
6. Preserve the existing Scope Lock, approval-ready handoff, single focused verification, Git safety and production/VPS safeguards from `AGENTS.md` / `AI_INSTRUCTIONS.md`. Updating memory is **not** authorization for unrelated source work, Git push, new phase, release, or deployment. Ask for fresh approval when those gates apply.
7. For documentation or memory-only updates, update the appropriate memory entry once and **do not recurse** by generating further memory-only revisions. Do not run irrelevant app test suites just to edit project instructions.

## Cross-IDE entry points

- **Shared rule:** this `.ai-rules` file. It is a readable convention, **not** itself an auto-loaded instruction format in every IDE.
- **Google Antigravity and other AGENTS-aware coding agents:** root `AGENTS.md` is the native always-on workspace entry; it explicitly points to this file and `MEMORY.md`.
- **VS Code with GitHub Copilot:** `.github/copilot-instructions.md` is the native repository instruction entry and points here. Other VS Code coding extensions may need explicit configuration to read project rules.
- **Continuity procedure:** commit/publish only within authorization, safely fast-forward pull the latest `main` in the other workspace, then instruct the new agent to read `AGENTS.md`, `.ai-rules` and `MEMORY.md` before resuming. Use the current repo state as evidence; never claim cross-IDE state updated before the files actually synchronize.
